sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/thompson1917-ch08-08> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "A particular arrangement of cell partitions at the eight-celled stage is not a specific or constant character of an organism; within a single species such as the frog, several essentially different arrangements occur.";
    dc:type "empirical-generalization";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "The arrangement of the first eight cells is not a constant character of a species";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "D’Arcy Wentworth Thompson, On Growth and Form (1st ed., Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1917), Chapter VIII, “The Forms of Tissues, or Cell-Aggregates (continued)”; topic “Eight-cell arrangements as specific characters”. Scope: eight-celled stage of eggs, especially the animal pole of the frog’s egg"
}
